<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Huang Daopo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1245–<a href="/years/1330">1330</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Huang Daopo was a Chinese textile artist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan dynasty</a> who rose from poverty to become one of the best-known women of the early Chinese textile industry. Sold into marriage by her family, she ran away from Songjiang, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a>, and sailed to Yazhou on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hainan">Hainan</a>, where she learnt spinning and weaving from the local Li people. Around 1295 she returned to Songjiang and taught local women <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> spinning and weaving, improving the tools for ginning, fluffing, spinning and weaving, among them a three-spindle treadle-powered machine. Her methods made her hometown a centre of textile manufacturing and had a large economic impact on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangtze-river">Yangtze River</a> Delta.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By tradition the weaver <b>Huang Daopo</b>, who had fled a marriage her family sold her into, learned cotton spinning on Hainan and around 1295 taught it to the women of Songjiang.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
